5. ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLES

All XSUS systems must follow common architectural principles.

5.1 Modularity

Each system must be divided into well-defined modules.

A module should have one primary responsibility and a clear interface.

5.2 Replaceability

Implementations must be replaceable without unnecessarily changing dependent systems.

External providers, models, databases, and infrastructure must be accessed through controlled interfaces.

5.3 Ownership

XSUS should own the source code, architecture, configuration, and operational documentation of its critical systems.

5.4 Interoperability

Systems must communicate through documented APIs, events, contracts, or adapters.

5.5 Security by Design

Security requirements must influence architecture and implementation from the beginning.

5.6 Evidence-Based Development

A feature is not considered verified merely because its code exists.

Verification requires appropriate tests, validation, and observable evidence.

5.7 Reproducibility

Builds, tests, deployments, and data migrations should be reproducible.

5.8 Incremental Scaling

The architecture must support gradual growth without introducing unnecessary infrastructure complexity too early.

5.9 Product Independence

Each user-facing XSUS product must have its own specialized engine and clearly defined responsibilities.

A product must not depend on another product merely because both belong to XSUS Studio.

5.10 Shared Intelligence

Products may use XSUS AI, Hyper XSUS Core, XSUS Language, XSUS Knowledge, and other shared services through documented interfaces.

Shared intelligence must not create unnecessary coupling between product engines.

5.11 Independent Product Operation

XSUS App, XSUS Web, XSUS Game, XSUS Logo, and XSUS Domain must be designed as independent products.

Users must be able to access their product workflows without first opening the XSUS AI application.

They may use XSUS AI in the background when intelligence assistance is required.

5.12 Clear System Boundaries

Every system must define:

- What it owns.
- What it provides.
- What it consumes.
- What it must never control.
- Which interfaces it exposes.
- Which dependencies it accepts.

5.13 Backward Compatibility

Public contracts and APIs must be versioned.

Changes that break existing consumers must be introduced through a controlled migration strategy.

5.14 Failure Isolation

A failure in one product must not automatically cause failures in unrelated products.

Shared services must use appropriate timeouts, error handling, isolation, and recovery mechanisms.

