7. MODEL INDEPENDENCE STRATEGY

XSUS AI must progressively reduce permanent dependence on external model providers.

The strategy consists of several stages.

7.1 Provider-Independent Interfaces

All model communication must pass through defined model contracts.

No product or core service should directly depend on provider-specific request formats throughout its codebase.

7.2 Multiple Model Support

The system should support multiple models according to task, language, performance, cost, and availability.

7.3 Independent Model Execution

Introduce locally executable models and self-hosted inference where technically and economically practical.

7.4 Model Adaptation

Research model adaptation, fine-tuning, specialized datasets, and task-specific models.

All training and adaptation must respect the relevant licenses and data rights.

7.5 Specialized XSUS Models

Develop specialized models for clearly defined tasks where there is sufficient data, evaluation capability, and operational justification.

7.6 Foundation Model Research

Long-term research may explore the development of proprietary foundation models.

This stage requires substantial data, compute, research expertise, evaluation, funding, and operational capacity.

It must not be treated as an immediate implementation requirement.

7.7 Model Evaluation

Every supported model should be evaluated using measurable criteria:

- Accuracy.
- Reliability.
- Latency.
- Resource consumption.
- Language quality.
- Task performance.
- Safety.
- Cost.
- Failure rate.

Model selection must be based on measured results rather than assumptions.

