18. ARCHITECTURAL GOVERNANCE

This section establishes rules that apply to all future XSUS development.

18.1 System Ownership

Every major module must have a defined owner within the architecture.

18.2 Interface-First Integration

Systems must define their communication contracts before tightly integrating their internal implementations.

18.3 No Hidden Dependencies

A module must not rely on undocumented behavior from another module.

18.4 No Unverified Completion

Implementation, testing, verification, and production readiness are separate milestones.

18.5 Controlled Expansion

New modules must be introduced only when their purpose, interfaces, and dependencies are understood.

18.6 Independent Testing

Critical systems must be testable without requiring unrelated product applications.

18.7 Data and Model Portability

Data formats, model interfaces, and storage systems should support migration and replacement.

18.8 Security Review

Changes affecting authentication, authorization, execution, storage, or external integrations must include security considerations.

18.9 Architecture Decision Records

Major architectural decisions should be documented with:

- Context.
- Decision.
- Alternatives.
- Consequences.
- Migration requirements.

18.10 Blueprint Evolution

This document is a living architecture blueprint.

It must evolve as the project develops, but changes must preserve a coherent global direction.

